The attached image displays the physical characterization of Nano-11, a dendrimer-like α-D-glucan nanoparticle designed as a vaccine adjuvant. Panel (A) is a Transmission Electron Microscopy (TEM) image, showing spherical particles with an irregular, cauliflower-like surface, primarily single or in small aggregates. Panel (B) illustrates the size distribution determined by dynamic light scattering, indicating a Z-average hydrodynamic diameter of Nano-11 ranging from 75 to 80 nm, with particles uniformly distributed between 30 and 110 nm. This characterization is essential as particle size and surface properties influence their uptake by dendritic cells and subsequent immunostimulatory effects.